// CONFORMANCE-CASE
// spec: 16-aggregates
// facility: rule.agg.index-vec (element accesses are compared at run time; statically an element borrow is a borrow of the container, D-0221, CHG-0260); conf.vec-element-borrows-compared-at-run
// kind: negative
// expect: diag.aliasing-conflict (dynamic)
//
// Two exclusive borrows of `v[0]`: the flow analysis does not compare two
// accesses to a `Vec`'s elements (they are not places of its own, D-0221),
// so the program is accepted, and `[Index-Vec]`'s dynamic check finds the
// second borrow meeting the first, which is used after it. The text before
// D-0221 refuted it statically; both implementations always left it to the
// run (D-0219 finding 10). A borrow of the whole vector while `r1` lives —
// `&v`, `Vec::push(&mut v, …)` — is refuted statically, as before.

import std;

fn main()
{
    Vec<i32> v = Vec::new();
    Vec::push(&mut v, 1);
    Vec::push(&mut v, 2);
    auto r1 = &mut v[0];
    auto r2 = &mut v[0];
    *r2 = 3;
    *r1 = 5;
    printf("%v\n", v[0]);
}
